A||||4||ROB MAC||Deck lid straps||||1||
Z||000000||ROB MAC||05-06-2000||08:43 PM||||I just snapped the second of the two deck lid cable straps ( the ones that keep the lid from blowing up and snappin off the hinges ) Anyone found a cheap replacement idea ? Is there any tricks to removing them cause at first glance they look like they're riveted on.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Rodney||05-07-2000||07:37 AM||||I have done some R&D on making replacement cables. The cables are very expensive to have made and also the large rivits would need to be machined from stainless steel. The old ones would have to be drilled out and the new ones hammered in. Prices were very high to make kits. Maybe someday???<P>Rodney Dickman||||reg||1
Z||000002||TOM||05-08-2000||12:25 AM||||Why cant you just swag some cable into loops around the rivet after you get the old one off? Very cheap to do||||reg||1
Z||000003||TheFiero64||05-08-2000||04:45 PM||||hey Rodney if the straps break would your decklid struts deem these unnecessary?||||reg||5
Z||000004||theogre||05-08-2000||06:40 PM||||I don't know specifically which gas tubes Rodney uses but no, you shouldn't need the limiting straps with them.<P>I was looking at the straps today. Mine work, but here's an idea. A couple.....<P>There is a Kevlar cord available. You could swage a sleve on it to make eye's. Like the cable this could be done with rivits in place. Kevlar cord is stronger than steel and more flexible. If you size the eyes right you might could slip them over like string on a button. <P>You might could take a length of Kevlar or Nylon web fold the ends over anout an inch an a half and stitch it down. Then button hole the folded ends. You should be able to put this on w/o taking off the rivits.<P>Obviously neither would look exactly stock (Kevlar is usually a yellowish color) but it should fix the problem with minimal BS.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-08-2000).]||||reg||1
